&lt;p&gt;Pictured: me&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Hey all, I&#039;m &lt;span class=&quot;printuser avatarhover&quot;&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/barredowl&quot;  &gt;&lt;img class=&quot;small&quot; src=&quot;http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=6001754&amp;amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;amp;timestamp=1606810748&quot; alt=&quot;barredowl&quot; style=&quot;background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=6001754)&quot; /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/barredowl&quot;  &gt;barredowl&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;, a somewhat ambitious artist and turtleneck sweater enthusiast. As of now, I mostly draw via a little program by the name of Photopea. It&#039;s trash, sure, but it&#039;s &lt;em&gt;my&lt;/em&gt; trash, you know?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;&lt;span&gt;The Process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;&lt;span&gt;Pixel Art&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;So usually, when I&#039;m thinking of drawing an SCP, I usually tend towards a situation which I can easily display. For example, maybe the interview log in &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.scpwiki.com/scp-3980&quot;&gt;SCP-3980&lt;/a&gt;, or the gramophone from &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.scpwiki.com/scp-3939&quot;&gt;SCP-3939&lt;/a&gt;. If it&#039;s just a simple humanoid, I usually just draw their face. I occasionally just draw a character from the SCP, such as the Masquerade figure from &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.scpwiki.com/scp-4430&quot;&gt;SCP-4430&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;After I&#039;ve fully convinced myself that drawing that part of the SCP is going to be fun, I head over to a &lt;a href=&quot;https://lospec.com/palette-list&quot;&gt;palette list&lt;/a&gt; and select something that catches my eye (usually a four-color palette, since that&#039;s the easiest to handle in Paint 3D). I download it via a .txt file and head over to Paint 3D to select the individual colors using a hex code.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Then, I shift the canvas to an appropriate size. If it&#039;s smaller (i.e, a humanoid picture) I usually choose a 30x30 canvas. If it&#039;s larger (i.e a situation I want to illustrate) I shoot for a 60x60. Then, I get to drawing, expand the canvas to 300x300 once it&#039;s finished, and publish it here!&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;&lt;span&gt;Drawings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I usually just draw SCPs that I like, to be honest. Nothing really special. The only real fun tidbit I have to share about the process is that most of my drawings, if colored, are sampled from a &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giorgio_de_Chirico&quot;&gt;Giorgio de Chirico&lt;/a&gt; painting. You can find the painting sampled from &lt;a href=&quot;https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/1/1b/De_Chirico%27s_Love_Song.jpg&quot;&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;!&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;&lt;span&gt;Contact Information&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If you want to reach out to me, whether it be commissions, words of praise, or your deepest, darkest confessions, here are some of the sites you can contact me from!&lt;/p&gt;
